<section xml:id="sec_using-B" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Using Magnetic Fields</title>
	
<assemblage component="implementation">
  <title>Implementation Notes</title>
  <p>
	The activities in this section were designed to be implemented in a classroom, after students have completed:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_magnetic-field"/>
			</p>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_biot-savart"/>
			</p>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_calculating-b"/>
			</p>
		</li>
	</ul>
  </assemblage>

  <subsubsection>
	<title>Activities</title>
	
  </subsubsection>

<activity>
	<title>Warmup</title>

		<p>
	
	</p>
	<p>
		The two wires shown carry the same current <m>I</m>.  Indicate the direction of the magnetic field at each marked point.
	</p>
	<figure xml:id="fig_b-wires1">
			<title>Wires Figure</title>
				<caption>Two wires.</caption>
				<image source="B-Circuit.png" width = "50%" ><description>Two wires carry current upward.  Three points are distributed horizontally, one to the left of both wires, one to the right of both wires, and one in the middle.</description></image>
			</figure>
</activity>



	<activity xml:id="class_current-loop">
		<title>The Current Loop</title>
		<introduction>
			<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			A circular loop of wire carries a constant clockwise current <m>I</m>.
		</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					What direction is the magnetic field at the center of the loop?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<hint>
				<p>
					Try adding up the magnetic fields from the current at a few different points around the loop.
				</p>
			</hint>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					What strategy do you think you could use to find the magnitude of the magnetic field?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<alert>Chop:</alert> <m>vdq = \frac{ds}{dt}dq = ds\frac{dq}{dt} = Ids</m>
				</p>
				<p>
					<alert>Multiply:</alert> <m>d\vec{B} = \frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\frac{dq v}{R^2}(-\hat{z}) = \frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\frac{IRd\theta}{R^2}(-\hat{z})</m>
				</p>
				<p>
					<alert>Add:</alert> <m>\vec{B} = \int d\vec{B} = \frac{\mu_0}{2}\frac{I}{R}(-\hat{z})</m>
				</p>
				<definition>
		<title>Magnetic Field Created by a Current Loop</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>The magnitude of the magnetic field created by a circular loop of radius <m>R</m> carrying current <m>I</m> at its center is <me>\vec{B} = \frac{\mu_0}{2}\frac{NI}{R}</me>
				Here <m>N</m> is the number of coils in the loop: <m>N = 1</m> for a single loop.
				</p>

			<figure xml:id="fig_mag-loop">
			<title>Current Loop Figure</title>
    		<caption>The magnetic field created by a current loop.</caption>
    		<image source="LongWire.png" width = "100%" ><description>Figure shows the magnetic field lines of a circular current loop. One field line follows the axis of the loop. Very close to the wire, the field lines are almost circular, like the lines of a long straight wire.</description></image>
		</figure>
		
		</statement>
	</definition>	
			</solution>
		</task>
	</activity>

	<activity xml:id="class_wire-segment">
		<title>The Wire Segment</title>
		<introduction>
			<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			A wire segment of length <m>L</m> carries current <m>I</m> to the right.  What is the magnetic field above the center of the wire?
		</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sketch a diagram of the wire.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<alert>Chop</alert> the wire up into pieces with current <m>\vec{I}dx</m>.  Sketch at least one piece on your diagram.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<alert>Multiply</alert> using the Biot-Savart Law to find the magnetic field created by the small piece of the wire.  Sketch this <m>\vec{dB}</m> on your diagram.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<alert>Add</alert> the <m>\vec{dB}</m> pieces using an integral to find the net magnetic field!
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</activity>

<references>
		<biblio xml:id="ref_loop-b">
            <p>Current Loop Figure from <url href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/12-4-magnetic-field-of-a-current-loop">Open Stax</url></p>
        </biblio>
    </references>
	
